{
  "id": "30446_kotaeru",
  "headword": "{堪|こた}える",
  "reading": "こたえる",
  "romaji": "kotaeru",
  "part_of_speech": "verb-ichidan",
  "gloss": "to hold out; to withstand; to endure",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "to hold out; to withstand; to endure",
      "explanation": "To keep standing under pressure, attack, or hardship without giving way. Somewhat literary on its own; most learners meet it inside the compound verb {持|も}ち{堪|こた}える."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "30446_kotaeru_ex1",
      "japanese": "{敵|てき}の{攻撃|こうげき}によく{堪|こた}えた。",
      "english": "They held out well against the enemy's attack.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "30446_kotaeru_ex2",
      "japanese": "{古|ふる}い{橋|はし}が{台風|たいふう}の{風|かぜ}に{堪|こた}えた。",
      "english": "The old bridge withstood the typhoon winds.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "30446_kotaeru_ex3",
      "japanese": "{苦|くる}しい{時期|じき}によく{堪|こた}えたからこそ、{今|いま}の{成功|せいこう}がある。",
      "english": "It is precisely because they held out through the hard times that they are successful now.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    }
  ],
  "notes": "TRANSITIVITY: Intransitive. The thing withstood is marked with に ({攻撃|こうげき}に{堪|こた}える), and the one holding out is the subject.\n\nUSAGE:\nOn its own the verb sounds formal or written. In ordinary speech it appears mainly in {持|も}ち{堪|こた}える (to hold out, to weather something), which is far more common.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {堪|こら}える: to endure; to hold back — the same kanji read こらえる, used of putting up with pain or suppressing tears and anger\n- {耐|た}える: to bear; to withstand — the everyday word for enduring hardship or for a material standing up to stress\n\nNOTE ON こたえる:\nThe こたえる of {寒|さむ}さが{身|み}にこたえる ('the cold really gets to me') is a different verb, usually written {応|こた}える or in kana. Do not confuse it with this one.",
